That's a requirement from the tickets, not something the tests invented. A rating with no text is treated as an incomplete review, so it's skipped. It doesn't count toward the summary either.

Where the requirement comes from

S1-021 (Normalize Ingested Reviews): "Every successfully normalized Review must contain: 1. Review text. 2. Rating." The test examples it lists include "Missing required review text".
S2-007: names "missing review text" as an example reason for skipping a record, and adds that "unsuccessful records do not silently count as successfully analyzed data."
Where the code enforces it

review_normalization.py: normalize_review returns None when the text is blank, so the row is counted as skipped.
models.py: the reviews table has ck_review_text_not_empty, so the database rejects empty text even if something gets past normalization.
review_summary.py: the summary and Q&A both read from the same active_reviews query. Skipped rows were never stored, so they can't show up in either.
Why it makes sense for this product

Q&A only has text to work with. It answers questions using review text as evidence and cites review ids. A review with only a rating gives it nothing to quote.
The numbers stay consistent. The summary's "N reviews, average X" describes exactly the reviews the assistant can see. If rating-only reviews counted toward the average, the summary would describe data the Q&A can't use.
The trade-off
On Google Maps, many reviews have stars and no text. So your average can differ from the rating Google shows for the same place, and the review count will be lower. The ingestion run's skipped_count reports how many records were left out, and S2-007 surfaces that in the UI, so the gap isn't hidden.

What the test checks
In test_csv_import_flows_into_reviews_summary_and_qa_context, the row ,4 (a 4-star rating with no text) is skipped. That's why the average is 3.5 (from the 5 and the 2) rather than 3.67. The test pins the rule down so nobody changes it by accident.

If the team ever wants rating-only reviews to count toward the stars, that's a product decision. The summary and the Q&A would then describe different datasets, so it would need a spec change and a migration, not a tweak to the test.



